---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'home' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Conventional Comments Toolkit"
  description="Review comments that say what they expect. A browser extension that makes the Conventional Comments convention easy to follow, with platform-level enforcement on the roadmap.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="hero-g">
          <div class="stack g20">
            <span class="eyebrow acc">code review:</span>
            <h1>Is that feedback, or a dig?</h1>
            <p class="lede measure">In a code review, tone gets lost in transit. The same sentence can feel like a helping hand or a jab, depending on how the person reading it receives it. <strong>Conventional Comments</strong> solves this with an almost ridiculously simple idea: every comment says what kind of comment it is before saying anything else.</p>
            <div class="hero-btns">
              <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('labels', 'en')}>See the labels</a>
              <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
            </div>
            <p class="small"><a href="#why">Why would one word change anything? ↓</a></p>
          </div>

          <div class="ba">
            <div class="ba-row">
              <span class="ba-tag">received</span>
              <div>
                <p class="ba-txt">That variable name doesn't mean anything.</p>
                <p class="ba-note">Flat. No tone of voice, no facial expression. Read on a Friday evening after two days spent on the pull request, it can easily sound like: <em>you're sloppy.</em></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="ba-row ok">
              <span class="ba-tag">received</span>
              <div>
                <p class="ba-txt mono"><span class="tk-l">nitpick</span> <span class="tk-d">(non-blocking)</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> that variable name doesn't mean anything</p>
                <p class="ba-note"><b>Exactly the same words.</b> But <code>nitpick</code> is the reviewer saying: “I know this is small.” The sting disappears without softening the message.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk" id="why">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">the problem:</span>
          <h2>A code review is text. Just text.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">No shrug. No half-smile. No “just thinking out loud.” Two people, two screens, often two time zones, and sometimes two native languages. All the cues that would tell you how much a remark matters in conversation are gone. Only the words remain.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="hum">
          <div>
            <span class="hum-q">“Am I being told off?”</span>
            <p>The reviewer was in a hurry, so they kept it short. The author reads coldness where there was only brevity.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="hum-q">“Do I actually have to do this?”</span>
            <p>“This loop could be simpler.” A requirement or just an idea? The author can't tell, so they rewrite it. Two hours lost for nothing.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="hum-q">“Am I really that bad?”</span>
            <p>A junior developer gets fifteen comments. They don't see fifteen separate remarks. They see fifteen versions of “you can't do this job.” Twelve are minor. One actually matters.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="hum-q">“Maybe I should just say nothing.”</span>
            <p>A reviewer who doesn't want to upset anyone hedges every comment. Now nothing sounds important. The review is polite, vague, and useless.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="lede measure">None of these misunderstandings comes from a technical disagreement. They all come from the same thing: <strong>the reviewer's intent is nowhere in the text.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">the convention:</span>
          <h2>Every comment says what it is before saying what it has to say.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Conventional Comments is a convention published at <a href="https://conventionalcomments.org/">conventionalcomments.org</a>. The idea fits on one line: put a word in front of each comment to say what kind of remark it is. Optionally add, in parentheses, whether it blocks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="anno" style="max-width:600px">
          <pre class="line"><span class="tk-l">what kind</span> <span class="tk-d">(does it block)</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> what you have to say</pre>
        </div>

        <div class="scroller">
          <table class="t">
            <thead><tr><th>What the reviewer writes</th><th>What the author hears</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="m"><span class="tk-l">praise</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> the error handling here reads really well</td>
                <td class="d">Somebody took the time to notice what went right.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="m"><span class="tk-l">nitpick</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> this name could be shorter</td>
                <td class="d">It's minor — and the reviewer said so themselves.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="m"><span class="tk-l">question</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> why a 30&nbsp;s timeout here?</td>
                <td class="d">I'm being asked, not accused.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="m"><span class="tk-l">suggestion</span> <span class="tk-d">(non-blocking)</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> we could extract a method</td>
                <td class="d">An idea, not an instruction. My call.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="m"><span class="tk-l">issue</span> <span class="tk-d">(blocking)</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> the session token is never invalidated</td>
                <td class="d">This is serious — and at least that's clear.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="callout acc">
          <span class="ct">the thing to take away</span>
          <h4>The label doesn't change what you say. It tells the reader how to read it.</h4>
          <p>That's what sets Conventional Comments apart from the usual advice about “being kind in code reviews.” Nobody is asking you to soften every sentence, add emoji, or start with a compliment. You write what you were going to write. You simply put a word in front that makes your intent explicit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">and here's the clever part:</span>
          <h2>The effort falls on the reviewer, not the person receiving the comment.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">This is easy to miss at first. Choosing a label forces you to pause for a second and ask yourself what you're actually doing. And that second is often enough to defuse the comment before anyone reads it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="eff">
          <div>
            <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-nitpick)">🔍 nitpick</span>
            <p>Putting <code>nitpick</code> in front of your own remark means acknowledging: “this is small.” The reviewer disarms the comment before it reaches the author.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-praise)">🎉 praise</span>
            <p>People rarely remember to call out what went well. A button in a toolbar makes it easy. It's a simple mechanism, but it changes the tone of a review.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-issue)">🔨 issue</span>
            <p>Marking something as blocking means owning that judgment. No more “anyway, I'll let you decide,” which commits nobody and leaves the author alone with the uncertainty.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-decision)">🏁 decision</span>
            <p>Closing a point without acting on it means explaining why. A disagreement can't simply disappear in silence.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="split">
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">the objection:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">“This will make reviews toothless.”</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The opposite happens. Without the convention, reviewers who don't want to hurt anyone tend to soften everything: “I was wondering whether maybe we could possibly…” Soon nothing sounds important, and the real problem disappears among all the hedging.</p>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">With a label, you can be direct without being harsh. <code>issue (blocking): memory leak in the handler</code> — that's a category, not a judgment about the person who wrote the code. <strong>Directness becomes easier precisely because the intent is explicit.</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">and the proportion becomes visible:</span>
            <div class="prop">
              <div class="prop-row">
                <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-nitpick)">🔍 nitpick</span><span class="prop-n">12</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prop-row">
                <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-question)">❓ question</span><span class="prop-n">2</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prop-row">
                <span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-issue)">🔨 issue</span><span class="prop-n">1</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="small">Without labels, the same review simply shows up as <strong style="color:var(--fg-2)">“15 comments.”</strong> Nothing else. That bare number is what can discourage a junior developer — not the comments themselves.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">making it stick:</span>
          <h2>Good habits fade fast.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">That's where this toolkit comes in — and only there. It doesn't teach people how to behave. It simply makes the habit easy to keep once nobody is consciously thinking about it anymore.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="duo">
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--fg-muted)">as you write</span>
            <h3>An extension that does the typing</h3>
            <p>A row of buttons sits above the comment box on GitHub. Type <code>?n</code> and the extension inserts <code>nitpick:</code>. Hover over a button and it reminds you what the label means. Choosing the right label becomes effortless — which is exactly why people are still doing it weeks later.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--accent)">once it's published</span>
            <h3>Blocking points stay visible</h3>
            <p>The extension keeps track of unresolved blocking comments directly on the pull request and shows how many remain. Nobody has to scroll back through the thread to check.</p>
            <p class="guar">It isn't there to police anyone. It's a safety net: <strong style="color:var(--fg)">an important point doesn't get lost somewhere in the thread.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="small measure">The extension is all you need to get started. The convention itself needs no tooling at all. The tooling simply makes it easier to use consistently.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="split">
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">the direction:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">Assist and warn are available today. Enforce comes next.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The extension already covers the first two stages. Enforce will eventually add an authoritative, platform-level merge gate, independent of the browser. That capability is on the roadmap.</p>
            <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('adoption', 'en')} style="align-self:flex-start">How teams adopt it</a>
          </div>
          <div class="traj">
            <div class="step" style="--st:var(--fg-faint)">
              <span class="sn">assist</span><span class="sw">available today</span>
              <span class="sd">The extension helps the people who install it. Nobody is blocked. No centralized tracking by default.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="step" style="--st:var(--warn)">
              <span class="sn">warn</span><span class="sw">available today</span>
              <span class="sd">The extension surfaces unresolved blocking points on the pull request without rejecting anything.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="step" style="--st:var(--block)">
              <span class="sn">enforce</span><span class="sw">on the roadmap</span>
              <span class="sd">Eventually, platform-level merge gates for GitHub, Azure DevOps, and other platforms could rely on the same rules. That capability isn't available yet, and its architecture isn't settled either.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="split">
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">privacy:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">Your comments never leave your browser.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">What people write in a code review can be more sensitive than the code itself. The extension reads and checks everything locally. Nothing is sent anywhere. No telemetry. No account. No stored token. It runs only on the domains you have explicitly allowed — github.com included — and nowhere else.</p>
            <p class="small"><strong style="color:var(--fg-2)">There is one exception:</strong> your own settings — such as language and shortcuts — can follow your browser's sync if you have it enabled. Settings only. Never review content.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stack g12">
            <span class="eyebrow">where it works:</span>
            <div class="plat">
              <div><span class="ph">GitHub</span><span class="pl">github.com<br>Enterprise Cloud · EMU<br>Enterprise Server</span></div>
              <div><span class="ph">Azure DevOps</span><span class="pl">Services<br>Server (self-hosted)<br><span style="color:var(--fg-faint)">with documented limitations</span></span></div>
              <div><span class="ph">Browsers</span><span class="pl">Chrome, Edge, Brave<br>Firefox<br>Open source · Apache-2.0</span></div>
            </div>
            <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('platforms', 'en')} style="align-self:flex-start">See the details</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>Start with one label in front of one sentence.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">That's it. Everything else — the extension and its settings — exists only to make that habit easy to keep.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('labels', 'en')}>See all 13 labels</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('adoption', 'en')}>Bring it to my team</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
